[gd_scene load_steps=23 format=3]

; Stadium level: an open-air ballpark, and the stand is set up on the field
; edge, right at the foot of the bleachers. Nothing here is roofed over - the
; only things above the crowd are sky, pennants and the top of the light mast.
;
; Framing. The camera is fixed in DaySimulation.tscn at (-9.89, 7.73, -9.65),
; pitched about 40 degrees down with a 62 degree vertical FOV on a 720x1280
; portrait viewport. Worked out, the ground it can see runs from about 2.6 m to
; 46 m away, and the top of the frame sits about 9 degrees below the horizon:
; nothing taller than about 13 m survives the top of the frame at distance.
; The visible floor is a narrow wedge running out from the stand, and inside
; that wedge the height a thing may reach is about (distance - 8.1) * 0.17.
; Everything that carries this place lives inside it: the mown field, the
; padded field wall at x = 7, and the bleacher bank stepping up and away behind
; the wall from x = 8.2 out to x = 14.8.
;
; The stand itself never moves. The Stand node lives in DaySimulation.tscn at
; (2.51, 0, -3.50) and every level scene is built around that transform. The
; front of the queue is 2.2 m down the stand's local -Z and every arrival route
; converges there, so the strip in front of the counter stays clear: the
; nearest geometry to the stand is open field.
;
; Every node is a direct child of the root, or of an unscaled group, with its
; own absolute transform. No mesh is ever parented under a scaled node - a
; child inherits that scale, which is what made an early version render slabs.

; --- Materials -------------------------------------------------------------

